   "title": "Control of Hodotermes mossambicus (Hagen) in the Karoo (Hodotermitidae, Isoptera)",
   "year": 1969,
   "date": "1969-01-01",
   "authors": [
    "H. van Ark"
   ],
   "venue": "Phytophylactica",
   "type": "article",
   "language": "en",
   "cited_by": 4,
   "oa_url": "",
   "volume": "1",
   "issue": "2",
   "pages": "41-50",
   "abstract": "Several insecticides were sprayed on the vegetation by means of a ground sprayer to determine their efficacy in controlling H. mossambicus. None gave effective control. However, 300 ml 18.5 par cent dieldrin E.C. per morgen applied aerially gave effective control of this species. The efficacy of the treatments was determined by means of surveys of the foraging holes. This procedure proved to be somewhat unsatisfactory. Two plots on which H. mossambicus populations had been controlled and two control plots were grazed according to a three-camp system. Plant surveys were carried out during Tune, 1963, 1965 and 1966. Completely resting the vegetation resulted in a greater improvement of the plant cover than grazing the vegetation according to a three-camp system. Control of the termites did not hasten the recovery of the plant cover. It is concluded that the damage done by H. mossambicus in the Aliwal North area is of a secondary nature and becomes serious only after the vegetation has- been destroyed by incorrect grazing practices. The damage is indirectly accentuated by adverse climatic conditions",
